"Find out whether the seasons come from Earth’s changing distance to the Sun or from the tilt of its axis.",
"<strong>Distance.</strong> Earth’s orbit is not quite a circle. Earth is about {#} AU from the Sun in early January and about {#} AU in early July ({#} AU, the astronomical unit, is Earth’s average distance from the Sun, about {#} million km). Sunlight spreads out as it travels, so its strength falls off as the square of the distance: twice as far means one quarter as much light on each square meter.",
"<strong>Tilt.</strong> Earth’s axis is tilted {#}° from upright compared with its orbit, and it keeps pointing the same way in space (toward Polaris) all year. In June the northern half of Earth leans toward the Sun; in December it leans away. Seen from the ground, the noon Sun climbs higher in the sky in June and stays lower in December.",
"<strong>Why height matters.</strong> A beam of sunlight that comes straight down lands on a small patch of ground. The same beam coming in at a low angle spreads over a larger patch, so each square meter gets less. A high Sun also means a longer day.",
